CorrNet: compute the CTC loss without cuDNN

Why: criterion_calculation passes CPU int32 targets, which makes current
PyTorch (2.11, NGC 26.02, cuDNN 9.19) route nn.CTCLoss to cuDNN's CTC kernel.
There it returns NaN gradients on most steps (runs diagnose-loss-004,
diagnose-nan-001..003): under AMP the GradScaler then halves its scale on
every step and upstream skips nearly every update, for pristine CorrNet as
much as for CVSign. Disabling cuDNN for the CTC call alone restores normal
training (scale settles at 256, no NaN after the first 5 calibration steps);
disabling it for the LSTM, BatchNorm or Conv3d modules instead does not.
PyTorch's native CTC kernel computes the same loss; nothing else changes.
Applies to: CorrNet @ 5a361cc709ae8f697f4c9e23760e64c2daa8f744
---
diff --git a/slr_network.py b/slr_network.py
index 9d49a0d..db14c05 100644
--- a/slr_network.py
+++ b/slr_network.py
@@ -129,6 +129,12 @@ class SLRModel(nn.Module):
         return loss
 
     def criterion_init(self):
-        self.loss['CTCLoss'] = torch.nn.CTCLoss(reduction='none', zero_infinity=False)
+        ctc_loss = torch.nn.CTCLoss(reduction='none', zero_infinity=False)
+
+        def ctc_loss_without_cudnn(*args):
+            with torch.backends.cudnn.flags(enabled=False):
+                return ctc_loss(*args)
+
+        self.loss['CTCLoss'] = ctc_loss_without_cudnn
         self.loss['distillation'] = SeqKD(T=8)
         return self.loss
